// Generated by tools/build-project-narration.mjs and tools/render-project-narration.py.
export const PROJECT_NARRATION_CATALOG=Object.freeze({"z-84c48915":{"text":"A leaf catching the light. An insect moving between flowers. The shade beneath a tree.","file":"z-84c48915.mp3"},"z-d6a2af1d":{"text":"Small things can invite us to pause and look closer.","file":"z-d6a2af1d.mp3"},"z-d7d734b5":{"text":"What catches your attention? What would you like to understand?","file":"z-d7d734b5.mp3"},"z-8d2349e0":{"text":"Technology shapes where we place our attention. What if it helped us notice more of the living world?","file":"z-8d2349e0.mp3"},"z-2cbb8d89":{"text":"NourishlandXR is a tool for exploring and learning through real places. Augmented reality brings digital information into your surroundings.","file":"z-2cbb8d89.mp3"},"z-e4dbbf5f":{"text":"Follow a question, share an observation, or explore a planting possibility , then look back at the place with another layer of understanding.","file":"z-e4dbbf5f.mp3"},"z-1dcecc46":{"text":"This demo uses prepared examples in the space around you. The Living Frame guides you through them.","file":"z-1dcecc46.mp3"},"z-65387f2d":{"text":"Your Control Panel keeps selected information and useful tools close by.","file":"z-65387f2d.mp3"},"z-c324440a":{"text":"Take your time. Continue when you are ready, and use Back to revisit a step.","file":"z-c324440a.mp3"},"z-f6c3eb3d":{"text":"A garden can become an outdoor classroom. A walk can reveal connections between plants, wildlife and the people who care for a place.","file":"z-f6c3eb3d.mp3"},"z-5a2e81b0":{"text":"Plant Orbs open plant information. Notes hold observations and messages. Areas and Totems help people find their way.","file":"z-5a2e81b0.mp3"},"z-5821b46c":{"text":"Learning Pathways connect discoveries with questions and activities. Together, these tools let a place grow with shared knowledge.","file":"z-5821b46c.mp3"},"z-ca59112e":{"text":"Choose an open space and place a Plant Orb. This spatial tag connects what you learn to a position in your surroundings.","file":"z-ca59112e.mp3"},"z-4b0b29a8":{"text":"Place the first Plant Orb in a comfortable spot in front of you. In a real place, its Orb would sit beside the plant.","file":"z-4b0b29a8.mp3"},"z-a0073c57":{"text":"Your Orb is placed. This sample represents Pigeon Pea, a shrub grown for food and its useful roles in a garden.","file":"z-a0073c57.mp3"},"z-c59d1767":{"text":"Select the Orb to open its Tag, then unfold Curiosity to follow its connected information.","file":"z-c59d1767.mp3"},"z-556d341c":{"text":"Tag gives you the plant's identity at a glance. Continue to unfold Curiosity and discover the connected information cells.","file":"z-556d341c.mp3"},"z-3d53b765":{"text":"There are many ways to get to know a plant. You might wonder how it grows, what it can provide, how it supports other life, or why it suits a particular place.","file":"z-3d53b765.mp3"},"z-f522c40f":{"text":"Let's follow one of those questions and see where it leads.","file":"z-f522c40f.mp3"},"z-3f964faa":{"text":"Select Culinary to discover the food examples.","file":"z-3f964faa.mp3"},"z-12b9427":{"text":"Select Fresh peas to see one way Pigeon Pea can be used.","file":"z-12b9427.mp3"},"z-611f0251":{"text":"You can keep exploring, or take a closer look at the related image.","file":"z-611f0251.mp3"},"z-ad0cba25":{"text":"Want a closer look? The Image panel shows the image connected to your selection. Its label and the Control panel label are highlighted so you can find them. Use the Image button on the Control panel to show or hide the picture.","file":"z-ad0cba25.mp3"},"z-6c50f472":{"text":"You have explored Tag, Curiosity and the Fruit Discovery Window. Your Orbs are now tucked away to make room for the next step.","file":"z-6c50f472.mp3"},"z-2a5ffcd5":{"text":"The Control Panel footer brings elements back when you want them. Green outlines mean visible; red outlines mean hidden. Notes and Totems join this row as you meet them.","file":"z-2a5ffcd5.mp3"},"z-16ac44cf":{"text":"Controls opens the options for the object you select. Fruit Discovery Window has its own simple controls.","file":"z-16ac44cf.mp3"},"z-6eaf6056":{"text":"Every plant brings a different story. Place another Orb beside Pigeon Pea, then choose an Australian rainforest sample.","file":"z-6eaf6056.mp3"},"z-d58a203":{"text":"Place a plain Orb beside Pigeon Pea. Then choose a plant for it using the buttons inside the Living Frame.","file":"z-d58a203.mp3"},"z-6de00fab":{"text":"Imagine choosing from thousands of plants, each with its own story of food, ecology and place. These Elements turn a landscape into a world of discovery. Try Carambola, Vetiver grass, African Peach, Mamey Sapote, Blue Quandong, Finger Lime or Lemon Myrtle. Your choice gives this Orb its own plant information.","file":"z-6de00fab.mp3"},"z-8b56d804":{"text":"A plant profile tells part of a story. A Note lets someone leave an observation, a reminder or a message for another person to discover later.","file":"z-8b56d804.mp3"},"z-e5d54508":{"text":"Place this example Note beside the plants. In a real place, you could leave a seasonal observation or a useful message for the next visitor.","file":"z-e5d54508.mp3"},"z-e1083a96":{"text":"Select the Note to read it. Someone visiting later can discover the message where it belongs.","file":"z-e1083a96.mp3"},"z-8d027f1":{"text":"So far, we have explored plants, knowledge and a local message.","file":"z-8d027f1.mp3"},"z-c4db4fcb":{"text":"In a real place, these pieces can grow together.","file":"z-c4db4fcb.mp3"},"z-c275b0ed":{"text":"An Area keeps them organised, with a Totem as its welcome point.","file":"z-c275b0ed.mp3"},"z-93d5e1b3":{"text":"This Totem belongs to My area. Its signs help visitors find the plants and messages that belong here.","file":"z-93d5e1b3.mp3"},"z-6867ae26":{"text":"The Totem names the Area. Its signs point toward nearby plants and Notes, giving visitors a way to find what they want to explore.","file":"z-6867ae26.mp3"},"z-2a08e078":{"text":"A larger place can hold several Areas, each with its own stories. Add Second Area to see how visitors can find their way between them.","file":"z-2a08e078.mp3"},"z-a1a0649d":{"text":"Connect the Totems so each Area can point toward the other. Their plants and messages remain connected to their own locations.","file":"z-a1a0649d.mp3"},"z-b99d8c0":{"text":"Select the Second Area sign on My area. Its arrow points toward the destination, helping you see where to go next.","file":"z-b99d8c0.mp3"},"z-fad645e9":{"text":"Plants, observations and directions can become part of one connected place. Next, see how this can support exploring, learning and planning on any land.","file":"z-fad645e9.mp3"},"z-8f91c867":{"text":"Plants, Notes and Areas can connect knowledge to any land , from school gardens and university campuses to farms and public landscapes.","file":"z-8f91c867.mp3"},"z-65d1b609":{"text":"Explore what could grow here before anything is planted.","file":"z-65d1b609.mp3"},"z-f4ca701e":{"text":"Compare what to plant, the light, water, soil and climate each plant needs, and what it can provide , food, shade, habitat or support for the soil.","file":"z-f4ca701e.mp3"},"z-1145ace4":{"text":"Visitors, teachers and students can use these questions to learn about a place. People planning a planting can use that knowledge to make informed choices.","file":"z-1145ace4.mp3"},"z-3649692b":{"text":"Want to see how this can become a learning experience? Open Learning Pathways, or choose Finish without learning example in the Control Panel.","file":"z-3649692b.mp3"},"z-3909fb41":{"text":"Explore a starting question, then connect two related LIMO cells. A new discovery opens between their ideas and can connect again to develop a deeper story.","file":"z-3909fb41.mp3"},"z-fed0aed":{"text":"Connect Sun & shade with Plants & forest layers to reveal Who shades whom? Then connect that discovery with First steps & later roles.","file":"z-fed0aed.mp3"},"z-6026cc40":{"text":"Select Uses in Pigeon Pea to choose the information for this learning example.","file":"z-6026cc40.mp3"},"z-a17fb6c3":{"text":"Hold Uses and Making until the connection completes.","file":"z-a17fb6c3.mp3"},"z-b2dbf266":{"text":"A teacher could ask which plant part is used and what preparation is recorded. The connected plant detail gives learners a source to return to.","file":"z-b2dbf266.mp3"},"z-1ffe23b1":{"text":"You have explored a small example. In a real place, plants, observations and local knowledge can grow together over time, creating something others can explore, learn from and contribute to.","file":"z-1ffe23b1.mp3"},"z-342f22bb":{"text":"Return to the welcome screen to discover a separate real garden project.","file":"z-342f22bb.mp3"},"z-c431c4ee":{"text":"Authored demonstration plant; not a field inventory.","file":"z-c431c4ee.mp3"},"z-f28fddda":{"text":"on the Living Map. This is an illustrative Area.","file":"z-f28fddda.mp3"},"z-2c4762dc":{"text":"Your discoveries remain saved in Learning cells. Continue to see how this learning belongs in a living place.","file":"z-2c4762dc.mp3"},"z-3edb70f6":{"text":"The scene is recovering. Your information is still available.","file":"z-3edb70f6.mp3"},"z-cfc3ec23":{"text":"No spatial content rendered before the startup deadline.","file":"z-cfc3ec23.mp3"},"z-a0a2eb1f":{"text":"A short guided demo of Plant Orbs, Areas and Notes.","file":"z-a0a2eb1f.mp3"},"z-e8c4b292":{"text":"Keep the demo open to return to the exact place you were exploring, or close it and return to the welcome screen.","file":"z-e8c4b292.mp3"},"z-67f5d4c5":{"text":"PEEK 3D is available inside the spatial experience.","file":"z-67f5d4c5.mp3"},"z-4e02be79":{"text":"Lean gently to see the depth. Press trigger or pinch to return to the scene.","file":"z-4e02be79.mp3"},"z-1486124":{"text":"PEEK 3D could not load. Please try again.","file":"z-1486124.mp3"},"z-45560771":{"text":"Spatial device immersive mode is not available here. Preview mode remains open.","file":"z-45560771.mp3"},"z-9111dd59":{"text":"Observe the ridges and the star shape of the opened fruit. Compare the flower, developing fruit and ripe fruit.","file":"z-9111dd59.mp3"},"z-7111b842":{"text":"Observe the outer skin, flesh and seed of a larger fruit. Hold the highlighted fruit with two pointers and pull apart to inspect the flesh and seed.","file":"z-7111b842.mp3"},"z-cc5ee184":{"text":"Observe the rounded fruit and its internal structure. Compare its flower-to-fruit development with the other examples.","file":"z-cc5ee184.mp3"},"z-7c74c6c5":{"text":"Observe the textured surface and the opened fruit. Compare the outer form and internal structure with Star fruit and Mamey sapote.","file":"z-7c74c6c5.mp3"},"z-a8e2b9d6":{"text":"Play development works for every fruit. Hold the fruit with two pointers and pull apart to inspect its halves and seeds. Choose another fruit below, or Continue.","file":"z-a8e2b9d6.mp3"},"z-673e302c":{"text":"PIMO is now closed so you can concentrate on fruit observation.","file":"z-673e302c.mp3"},"z-75dd9b39":{"text":"Choose Star fruit, Mamey sapote, African peach or Chinese bayberry on the main Control Panel. Each fruit brings its own botanical picture and observation information to the panels.","file":"z-75dd9b39.mp3"},"z-4e5ef221":{"text":"Compare the flower, developing fruit and ripe fruit. Hold the highlighted fruit with two pointers and pull apart to inspect its detail.","file":"z-4e5ef221.mp3"},"z-d12f9757":{"text":"Look closely at the plant, its flowers and its fruit.","file":"z-d12f9757.mp3"},"z-483c6737":{"text":"Point at an arrowed pod and hold Trigger to carry it. Hold it with the second pointer too, then pull your hands apart to open it. Use Play development in the window controls to follow flower-to-fruit development.","file":"z-483c6737.mp3"},"z-fd490a20":{"text":"Choose fruits on the main Control Panel. Hold both box grips to move it; joystick up/down adjusts distance. Release beside the panel to dock.","file":"z-fd490a20.mp3"},"z-5aece3ef":{"text":"You can play as you explore: move the Hero Dice, catch a butterfly, or try the controls whenever you like.","file":"z-5aece3ef.mp3"},"z-bb57d2e6":{"text":"Begin with what can be observed in this place. General plant information becomes more useful when it is checked against season, light, water, soil and the plant in front of you.","file":"z-bb57d2e6.mp3"},"z-933321fa":{"text":"Follow the living logic. Ask how the plant grows, what it depends on, what it supports and which parts of the information remain uncertain.","file":"z-933321fa.mp3"},"z-53fd7f20":{"text":"A fact becomes practical when it serves a purpose. Seed might become food, another plant, a shared resource or part of a wider planting design.","file":"z-53fd7f20.mp3"},"z-1c405d55":{"text":"Turn the purpose into a small decision. Record what happens, compare it with the intention and adjust when the place gives new feedback.","file":"z-1c405d55.mp3"},"z-571f95d8":{"text":"Add a second plant to apply the reasoning in the mapped place.","file":"z-571f95d8.mp3"},"z-17903f6f":{"text":"Continue through the next way of thinking.","file":"z-17903f6f.mp3"},"z-b50cf0c6":{"text":"Connected learning moves from “what is this?” to “what could I do with this here?”","file":"z-b50cf0c6.mp3"},"z-1ccdbaaf":{"text":"Follow the connection through observation, understanding, purpose and action.","file":"z-1ccdbaaf.mp3"},"z-5a31b117":{"text":"Follow this sign to the neighbouring Totem.","file":"z-5a31b117.mp3"},"z-f87d9599":{"text":"Signs are visible. Each directional plaque points toward nearby Orbs, Notes or Totems.","file":"z-f87d9599.mp3"},"z-36ce4018":{"text":"Signs are hidden. Turn them on to follow directional plaques to nearby Orbs, Notes or Totems.","file":"z-36ce4018.mp3"},"z-4fb615c5":{"text":"Totem faded. Use Fade again to restore its full visibility.","file":"z-4fb615c5.mp3"},"z-ef1333dc":{"text":"Totem restored. Fade dims it temporarily while you explore nearby information.","file":"z-ef1333dc.mp3"},"z-8a88869c":{"text":"A Project holds information for a real place. This demonstration uses prepared samples in the room around you.","file":"z-8a88869c.mp3"},"z-821fa89b":{"text":"This plant profile also has a standard web view. Keep exploring here, or open that view when you choose.","file":"z-821fa89b.mp3"},"z-40f33229":{"text":"Grip moves panels and objects. Trigger interacts with buttons and cells.","file":"z-40f33229.mp3"},"z-5f9a5c2b":{"text":"3D garden. Place three Totems: entrance, open forest, then swales.","file":"z-5f9a5c2b.mp3"},"z-c4c587b8":{"text":"Desktop preview: drag horizontally to turn, vertically to tilt. In XR, grip both opposite edges.","file":"z-c4c587b8.mp3"},"z-a42401f1":{"text":"Explore the visible cells for more detail.","file":"z-a42401f1.mp3"},"z-bbf8ab8c":{"text":"Aim at the highlighted position and confirm placement.","file":"z-bbf8ab8c.mp3"},"z-67bb73da":{"text":"Tag gives you a compact identity for this plant. Continue to unfold its Curiosity cells.","file":"z-67bb73da.mp3"},"z-8027c1e0":{"text":"Your chosen plant now has its own PIMO. Explore its information, or continue to add a sample Note.","file":"z-8027c1e0.mp3"},"z-e292e33a":{"text":"An optional field question route. Branch or pause at any time.","file":"z-e292e33a.mp3"},"z-f75d687":{"text":"Continue exploring, revisit a topic or record something you noticed about this place.","file":"z-f75d687.mp3"},"z-975b588e":{"text":"Your place is saved. Resume when you choose, restart this path, or continue exploring freely.","file":"z-975b588e.mp3"},"z-37bd2b20":{"text":"Follow a gentle sequence of connected topics, or continue exploring freely. More paths are being developed.","file":"z-37bd2b20.mp3"},"z-14293666":{"text":"Take a moment to record something you noticed about this place. Your observation can become a Note that you return to later.","file":"z-14293666.mp3"},"z-96064dad":{"text":"Understand This Place offers an optional route through seven connected topics. You can leave the path, explore any other topic and return whenever you choose.","file":"z-96064dad.mp3"},"z-af7d1b9d":{"text":"Aim beside the place and press the circle once. You can cancel and return to the path.","file":"z-af7d1b9d.mp3"},"z-20b1be9":{"text":"A short demonstration of how a learning package can guide exploration without taking over the main demo.","file":"z-20b1be9.mp3"},"z-634be785":{"text":"Begin with the landscape. Notice sun, slope, water, access and what already grows here.","file":"z-634be785.mp3"},"z-dce2cf67":{"text":"Arrange the first stages with succession in mind. Leave room for plants and relationships to change.","file":"z-dce2cf67.mp3"},"z-d8a0ebca":{"text":"A brief field-learning package for reading a forest through place, plants and relationships.","file":"z-d8a0ebca.mp3"},"z-774da458":{"text":"Start with landform, water, exposure and the broad structure of the forest.","file":"z-774da458.mp3"},"z-75b324e1":{"text":"Observe plant form, leaves, bark and growth habit. Keep uncertain identification marked as uncertain.","file":"z-75b324e1.mp3"},"z-b76fc3c1":{"text":"Look for habitat, pollinators, fungi, litter and other connections before describing the forest.","file":"z-b76fc3c1.mp3"},"z-18d5a604":{"text":"Complete the opening introduction to unlock these optional packages.","file":"z-18d5a604.mp3"},"z-8863240b":{"text":"Choose a brief demonstration package. It runs separately from the main tutorial.","file":"z-8863240b.mp3"},"z-de99af9b":{"text":"Package complete. This short cycle connected a goal, a few learning cells and guidance in the Control panel.","file":"z-de99af9b.mp3"},"z-59213e44":{"text":"Open Tools in the Control panel and choose End learning to return to the demo.","file":"z-59213e44.mp3"},"z-3447ad2a":{"text":"We hope you are in a calm place, with plenty of clear space around you.","file":"z-3447ad2a.mp3"},"z-40f584a0":{"text":"Get comfortable. Relax your shoulders, keep your movements gentle, and take your time.","file":"z-40f584a0.mp3"},"z-47da3780":{"text":"When you feel ready, let's imagine what this space could become.","file":"z-47da3780.mp3"},"z-5b6733d6":{"text":"Selected details and images open here. Use Settings to adjust the experience, Help for guidance, Back to revisit a page, or Hide for an unobstructed view.","file":"z-5b6733d6.mp3"},"z-7413acf9":{"text":"HINT · The image panel opens above this panel.","file":"z-7413acf9.mp3"},"z-f4143d06":{"text":"Select the Plant Orb to open its information. Hold controller grip to move it; release grip to place it. With hands, pinch to hold and release the pinch to place it.","file":"z-f4143d06.mp3"},"z-b480cce8":{"text":"Pigeon Pea now has a location in this scene. This Plant Orb connects information to this plant in the real place.","file":"z-b480cce8.mp3"},"z-ecc163ac":{"text":"The Orb is interactive. Press it to explore its information, beginning with simple facts and deeper connected branches.","file":"z-ecc163ac.mp3"},"z-6eb630b8":{"text":"Choose a plant in the Living Frame.","file":"z-6eb630b8.mp3"},"z-9f0aaca6":{"text":"Give your Orb its own living story.","file":"z-9f0aaca6.mp3"},"z-5d6e0ee7":{"text":"Select a plant in the Living Frame.","file":"z-5d6e0ee7.mp3"},"z-3ec64a4e":{"text":"In a full Project, you can edit this Note or create your own. Notes can hold observations, instructions and other place-based knowledge.","file":"z-3ec64a4e.mp3"},"z-edd8a616":{"text":"Select a sign to find one of the sample plants or Note.","file":"z-edd8a616.mp3"},"z-a1301ea2":{"text":"Select a sign to see where it points.","file":"z-a1301ea2.mp3"},"z-42aa346e":{"text":"Point at the Second Area signage on your first Totem, then press the trigger.","file":"z-42aa346e.mp3"},"z-b1639943":{"text":"The neighbouring Totem will appear. Its sign gives you a direction through the space.","file":"z-b1639943.mp3"},"z-64328553":{"text":"Select the Second Area sign. Continue is an alternative.","file":"z-64328553.mp3"},"z-a6ff6cbf":{"text":"Follow its sign to move through the real space. Each Totem welcomes you to its own Area, plants and Notes.","file":"z-a6ff6cbf.mp3"},"z-961a0b6d":{"text":"Point at a sign to highlight its destination.","file":"z-961a0b6d.mp3"},"z-99a26c4e":{"text":"The Areas are linked. Each Totem still keeps its own local plants and Notes.","file":"z-99a26c4e.mp3"},"z-52e52454":{"text":"Let's test adding these elements to the landscape.","file":"z-52e52454.mp3"},"z-71fff8fc":{"text":"Aim at the wooden ring and squeeze to move the map. Move your hand to carry it; use the stick to turn it or adjust distance. With tracked hands, pinch the ring. Release to leave it in place.","file":"z-71fff8fc.mp3"},"z-93981b7a":{"text":"Carry the Totem onto the bright gold landing circle and release.","file":"z-93981b7a.mp3"},"z-471648be":{"text":"The next Totem is ready beside the Living Frame.","file":"z-471648be.mp3"},"z-f81b552f":{"text":"Watch the three Orbs appear before placing the swale Totem.","file":"z-f81b552f.mp3"},"z-600bc330":{"text":"Your three Totems connect one living place. Continue when ready.","file":"z-600bc330.mp3"},"z-84fa637b":{"text":"Totem 2 plant set is out of sync.","file":"z-84fa637b.mp3"},"z-fdc2c027":{"text":"Pigeon Pea and the learning mesh are available together. Their existing cells can form one connection.","file":"z-fdc2c027.mp3"},"z-cd53d16f":{"text":"Continue to show the plant source and learning target together.","file":"z-cd53d16f.mp3"},"z-bdd5395f":{"text":"The main action uses Uses. You can choose another prepared example here instead.","file":"z-bdd5395f.mp3"},"z-f464af32":{"text":"Pigeon Pea is unavailable. Return to its Plant Orb and try again.","file":"z-f464af32.mp3"},"z-4b6f2271":{"text":"The learning target is unavailable. Return to the pathway and try again.","file":"z-4b6f2271.mp3"},"z-be448812":{"text":"The two highlighted cells remain connected while you continue.","file":"z-be448812.mp3"},"z-162bcbef":{"text":"Select the learning cell again to retry.","file":"z-162bcbef.mp3"},"z-8961a754":{"text":"Six starting questions surround the Living Frame. Connect related cells to reveal ten new investigations, then combine discoveries to develop a deeper story.","file":"z-8961a754.mp3"},"z-69cbd57e":{"text":"Explore a starting question, then connect ideas around the Living Frame.","file":"z-69cbd57e.mp3"},"z-fbbf7a0b":{"text":"Hold a starting cell to explore it, or continue to connect two LIMO cells.","file":"z-fbbf7a0b.mp3"},"z-8b042a3":{"text":"Choose one of ten combinations in the Control panel. Connect the two highlighted cells and watch a new discovery unfold around the Living Frame.","file":"z-8b042a3.mp3"},"z-4ec69f9f":{"text":"Start with Who shades whom? Then connect that discovery with First steps & later roles to explore how this place could change.","file":"z-4ec69f9f.mp3"},"z-76ec019e":{"text":"Select a combination, then connect its two source cells.","file":"z-76ec019e.mp3"},"z-43c77e14":{"text":"On a first visit, the map answers immediate questions: What is this? Why is it here? What can I notice or do next?","file":"z-43c77e14.mp3"},"z-6994f774":{"text":"For a school, the same place becomes a learning environment where students can observe, compare, record and return over time.","file":"z-6994f774.mp3"},"z-6dd18d86":{"text":"For a garden, park or land steward, published knowledge, visitor guidance and local observations remain organised around the real landscape.","file":"z-6dd18d86.mp3"},"z-8d96d2a":{"text":"Select the Note to discover how observations stay connected to a place.","file":"z-8d96d2a.mp3"},"z-b0fd28ae":{"text":"Your observation Note is anchored to this place.","file":"z-b0fd28ae.mp3"},"z-fecf4253":{"text":"Your Note is anchored here: a discovery another visitor can return to.","file":"z-fecf4253.mp3"},"z-96860253":{"text":"Look around slowly. The centre aim will appear when you are ready.","file":"z-96860253.mp3"},"z-100f0d93":{"text":"Aim the upright preview where the Totem should stand. Use the thumbstick to adjust depth.","file":"z-100f0d93.mp3"},"z-11811a8e":{"text":"Take in the space before choosing the next position.","file":"z-11811a8e.mp3"},"z-382d83a4":{"text":"A Plant Orb gives a plant's information a location in the scene.","file":"z-382d83a4.mp3"},"z-f264c7f6":{"text":"Pigeon Pea will be our first example. No previous plant knowledge is needed.","file":"z-f264c7f6.mp3"},"z-636a718a":{"text":"Aim the upright ghost where the Totem should stand. Adjust its distance with the controller thumbstick, then confirm placement.","file":"z-636a718a.mp3"},"z-39bb2669":{"text":"The prepared message describes the two sample profiles.","file":"z-39bb2669.mp3"},"z-61fa1d5d":{"text":"This prepared profile demonstrates plant information. A real project can use its own plant profiles.","file":"z-61fa1d5d.mp3"},"z-24b33416":{"text":"A Totem gives an Area a clear welcome point for its signs and local information.","file":"z-24b33416.mp3"},"z-4f179446":{"text":"Aim the upright preview where the Totem should stand. Adjust depth with the joystick, then press the trigger.","file":"z-4f179446.mp3"},"z-d4f8f756":{"text":"Choose a location and press the trigger to place the Note.","file":"z-d4f8f756.mp3"},"z-bc93a803":{"text":"Choose a location and tap the aiming circle to place the Note.","file":"z-bc93a803.mp3"},"z-582bcd0a":{"text":"Aim at a visible plant information cell to explore it.","file":"z-582bcd0a.mp3"},"z-9c3ba86d":{"text":"You have explored this plant information. Continue when you are ready.","file":"z-9c3ba86d.mp3"},"z-e88de40a":{"text":"Returned to the previous group of plant information.","file":"z-e88de40a.mp3"},"z-ab440a86":{"text":"Use the Notes button to create a new Note.","file":"z-ab440a86.mp3"},"z-90366a6":{"text":"Choose a rainforest plant for your second Orb.","file":"z-90366a6.mp3"},"z-71b46916":{"text":"Grip a Note card to move it. Stick left/right turns it; up/down adjusts distance. The corner button minimizes the Note.","file":"z-71b46916.mp3"},"z-632e5509":{"text":"Totem text editing is available in the creator tools. Use Controls to try its light and signage.","file":"z-632e5509.mp3"},"z-da78bb5f":{"text":"Move your phone briefly, then tap the circle again.","file":"z-da78bb5f.mp3"},"z-f2a479bd":{"text":"A small observation can become useful knowledge over time.","file":"z-f2a479bd.mp3"},"z-e50d9585":{"text":"A prepared message about the two sample plant profiles.","file":"z-e50d9585.mp3"},"z-f9dd478e":{"text":"Demo WebGL program could not be created.","file":"z-f9dd478e.mp3"},"z-47aed18a":{"text":"If this remains visible, exit AR and reopen the demo.","file":"z-47aed18a.mp3"},"z-2434179f":{"text":"The AR demo is not designed for desktop use. It needs a compatible phone or headset for spatial tracking and interaction with the environment.","file":"z-2434179f.mp3"},"z-3dd5a045":{"text":"On desktop, our existing book-style prototype explains how places, plant information and learning connect. There is no simulated AR mode.","file":"z-3dd5a045.mp3"},"z-73ee8acc":{"text":"Select a Note, select a Plant Orb, then re-select the Note. Try turning it with the stick, adding a widget and minimizing it.","file":"z-73ee8acc.mp3"},"z-95034e45":{"text":"Place the first Totem at the entrance. It will give visitors a starting point to explore this place.","file":"z-95034e45.mp3"},"z-82d93568":{"text":"An entrance and starting point has been added. Visitors can begin here and follow the connected Totems through the landscape.","file":"z-82d93568.mp3"},"z-e3915e10":{"text":"A checkpoint with information about this Area has been added. Its plant Orbs help visitors discover what grows here and how the plants connect.","file":"z-e3915e10.mp3"},"z-3d0d05de":{"text":"A checkpoint has been added at the swale entrance. Visitors can follow the route to learn about water, planting and the work taking place in this Area.","file":"z-3d0d05de.mp3"},"z-c55f74c9":{"text":"Thousands of plants. Thousands of living stories.","file":"z-c55f74c9.mp3"},"z-e120e2a9":{"text":"Choose Blue Quandong, Finger Lime or Lemon Myrtle. Your second Orb becomes that plant, ready to explore.","file":"z-e120e2a9.mp3"},"z-c202490e":{"text":"A tall eastern Australian rainforest tree whose vivid blue fruit connects canopy-feeding birds and forest-floor seed dispersers.","file":"z-c202490e.mp3"},"z-1343e8b3":{"text":"PlantNET records Blue Quandong in riverine and lowland subtropical rainforest on alluvium and along streams, north from the Nambucca River.","file":"z-1343e8b3.mp3"},"z-539237b7":{"text":"Leaves are simple, oblong to elliptic and regularly toothed. The globose blue fruit surrounds a deeply sculptured stone. NSW Flora Online notes that some treatments include this species in the broader Elaeocarpus angustifolius.","file":"z-539237b7.mp3"},"z-5b0ae7ad":{"text":"A thorny rainforest-understorey citrus with tiny distinctive leaves, finger-shaped fruit and a documented butterfly-host relationship.","file":"z-5b0ae7ad.mp3"},"z-ccb8a390":{"text":"Australian Finger Lime is native to rainforest communities from south-east Queensland to north-east New South Wales, where it grows as an understorey shrub or small tree.","file":"z-ccb8a390.mp3"},"z-6eec9343":{"text":"The shrub bears solitary axillary spines and small aromatic leaves. Its narrow cylindrical fruit contains separate bead-like juice vesicles; shape and colour vary among forms.","file":"z-6eec9343.mp3"},"z-e501c193":{"text":"A fragrant Queensland rainforest tree whose flowering season can turn a plant profile into a question about who visits flowers, and when.","file":"z-e501c193.mp3"},"z-5bd51f5":{"text":"The Australian National Botanic Gardens records Lemon Myrtle in Queensland coastal forests from Brisbane to Mackay, as a shrub or tree reaching about 8 metres.","file":"z-5bd51f5.mp3"},"z-23b35beb":{"text":"Leaves are strongly lemon-scented. Numerous white flowers with conspicuous stamens occur in clusters; nut-like capsules contain several small seeds.","file":"z-23b35beb.mp3"},"z-5cc7ac95":{"text":"What if the food forest is not only productive, but also begins to read like a local forest? Blue Quandong opens that next layer: a long-lived, large tree associated with eastern Australian rainforest and stream corridors.","file":"z-5cc7ac95.mp3"},"z-b8d9ede2":{"text":"Think in decades, not just harvests. A Blue Quandong can become a large tree; place it where mature height, buttressed roots, shade and access to water will fit. It is a forest-scale choice, not a compact orchard tree.","file":"z-b8d9ede2.mp3"},"z-374b83d6":{"text":"PlantNET links this species with lowland and riverine subtropical rainforest, especially alluvial ground and stream margins. In a food forest, use that habitat clue to ask about moisture, soil movement and the other layers that belong beside a canopy tree.","file":"z-374b83d6.mp3"},"z-cc42507":{"text":"Rainforest records describe fruit-doves and other birds feeding in the canopy. Fallen fruit is also used by ground-foraging animals, including brush-turkeys and cassowaries. The fruit links more than one forest height to the same tree.","file":"z-cc42507.mp3"},"z-730952f":{"text":"Black-and-white rainforest scene: Blue Quandong fruit, birds and a cassowary on the forest floor.","file":"z-730952f.mp3"},"z-f4e7cf91":{"text":"In north Queensland, southern cassowaries eat Blue Quandong fruit and can carry its large seed away from the parent tree before depositing it. This is a seed-dispersal relationship, not simply an animal eating a crop. Where cassowaries live, intact rainforest connections matter.","file":"z-f4e7cf91.mp3"},"z-217dc474":{"text":"The blue drupe has a thin fleshy layer around a hard, deeply sculptured stone; the stone contains seeds. The dramatic “quandong” surface is a protective endocarp, so the seed story begins by looking past the blue outside.","file":"z-217dc474.mp3"},"z-44eab667":{"text":"Black-and-white propagation plate showing Blue Quandong fruit, the deeply sculptured stone and a seedling.","file":"z-44eab667.mp3"},"z-f86587e9":{"text":"Look for simple oblong-to-elliptic leaves with regular teeth. NSW Flora Online accepts Elaeocarpus grandis and notes that some taxonomic treatments include it within the broader E. angustifolius concept. Keep the source and region attached when comparing records.","file":"z-f86587e9.mp3"},"z-b1190626":{"text":"Finger Lime changes the picture from a broad canopy tree to a thorny rainforest-understorey shrub. Add a native layer beneath taller food-forest trees and the planting begins to echo the structure of a subtropical forest.","file":"z-b1190626.mp3"},"z-84e83e82":{"text":"Its natural form is a shrub or small tree, so explore a mid- or understorey position with room for spiny branches, light and access. The food-forest design idea is to combine useful harvest with a native forest layer; actual fit depends on local climate and cultivar.","file":"z-84e83e82.mp3"},"z-7dea1b36":{"text":"Finger Lime is native to tropical-to-subtropical rainforest communities in south-east Queensland and north-east New South Wales. In those forests, the plant is one part of a layered community, not a stand-alone crop. Ask what canopy, shelter and neighbouring plants your site can provide.","file":"z-7dea1b36.mp3"},"z-9ca8d131":{"text":"The Dainty Swallowtail is recorded using Finger Lime as a host plant. Its caterpillar eats leaves: the relationship is with the living plant, not the fruit. Look closely before pruning, and treat a few chewed leaves as evidence of a wider food web rather than automatic damage.","file":"z-9ca8d131.mp3"},"z-f30169f":{"text":"Black-and-white engraving of thorny Finger Lime twigs, tiny leaves and a Dainty Swallowtail caterpillar feeding on a leaf.","file":"z-f30169f.mp3"},"z-3bfbfc4c":{"text":"The fruit is an elongated, narrow citrus berry. Inside, juice vesicles separate into small bead-like segments that release when the fruit is opened. Fruit length, skin colour and pulp colour vary among forms; the finger shape and vesicles are the signature, not a pod of large seeds.","file":"z-3bfbfc4c.mp3"},"z-9bdefdb2":{"text":"A seed-grown plant may differ from a named selection. Commercial growing guidance discusses seedling rootstocks and vegetative propagation; use the linked NSW guide for the method that matches your goal. Keep cultivar, source and propagation method with the record.","file":"z-9bdefdb2.mp3"},"z-47bb912e":{"text":"Black-and-white teaching illustration of finger lime seeds, tiny obovate leaves and thorny twig morphology.","file":"z-47bb912e.mp3"},"z-5077517c":{"text":"Finger Lime leaves are simple and alternate, and much smaller than the long leaves often used in generic citrus drawings. The attached reference shows their compact form; the twig's straight axillary spines are equally diagnostic. Compare several shoots because leaf shape varies.","file":"z-5077517c.mp3"},"z-82209a4e":{"text":"Lemon Myrtle brings a Queensland coastal-rainforest tree into the food-forest story. Its place is not only “the herb with a lemon scent”: it can become a fragrant woody layer, a flowering event and an invitation to observe seasonal visitors.","file":"z-82209a4e.mp3"},"z-7ec5188d":{"text":"The species can grow as a medium-sized shrub or tree. In a food forest, plan for its eventual size, shelter and moisture needs; use the foliage as a sensory connection, while keeping the broader canopy and neighbouring plants in view.","file":"z-7ec5188d.mp3"},"z-c85f559f":{"text":"Lemon Myrtle occurs naturally in Queensland coastal forests. The ANBG also grows it in a sheltered rainforest gully, noting protection from canopy and surrounding shrubs. That gives the food forest a useful design question: can this planting offer a similarly sheltered, moist-growing position?","file":"z-c85f559f.mp3"},"z-2f756444":{"text":"Clusters of white flowers carry many conspicuous stamens. Rather than assume which local species pollinates them, watch a flowering branch at different times of day: who visits, how long do they stay, and does activity change with weather? Add observations with date and place.","file":"z-2f756444.mp3"},"z-caf66588":{"text":"Black-and-white Lemon Myrtle flowering branch with small flower-visiting insects in a Queensland coastal rainforest setting.","file":"z-caf66588.mp3"},"z-d34e2590":{"text":"The fruit is a nut-like capsule with several small seeds. ANBG notes that capsules may retain their seeds until the fruit falls. Follow the linked growing guide before choosing a propagation method; record whether your material came from seed or a cutting.","file":"z-d34e2590.mp3"},"z-12907f25":{"text":"Black-and-white Lemon Myrtle teaching plate showing a flower cluster, capsule, small seeds and a rooted cutting.","file":"z-12907f25.mp3"},"z-176bd4b0":{"text":"Backhousia citriodora belongs to the myrtle family. Its strongly lemon-scented leaves are oval to lance-shaped; the flowers appear in long-stalked clusters, with numerous fluffy stamens. The scientific name points to the sensory feature without replacing careful plant identification.","file":"z-176bd4b0.mp3"},"z-22746f59":{"text":"Mark first buds, peak flowering, capsule development and leaf flush in dated Notes. Over time, the plant becomes a living calendar for this particular site, and the visitor can compare it with rainfall, temperature and nearby flowering species.","file":"z-22746f59.mp3"},"z-30db8e01":{"text":"Uma árvore alta das florestas tropicais do leste da Austrália, cujos frutos azuis ligam aves do dossel aos dispersores de sementes do solo.","file":"z-30db8e01.mp3"},"z-58e153b8":{"text":"Een hoge regenwoudboom uit oostelijk Australië. De blauwe vruchten verbinden vogels in de kroon met zaadverspreiders op de bosbodem.","file":"z-58e153b8.mp3"},"z-80010677":{"text":"E se a floresta alimentar não fosse apenas produtiva, mas começasse também a parecer uma floresta local? O quandong-azul abre essa camada seguinte: uma árvore de grande porte associada às florestas tropicais do leste da Austrália e aos corredores ribeirinhos.","file":"z-80010677.mp3"},"z-7eceb997":{"text":"Wat als het voedselbos niet alleen productief is, maar ook op een plaatselijk bos gaat lijken? De blauwe quandong opent die volgende laag: een grote boom uit regenwoud en beekcorridors in oostelijk Australië.","file":"z-7eceb997.mp3"},"z-c570e6ca":{"text":"Pense em décadas, não apenas em colheitas. O quandong-azul pode tornar-se uma árvore de grande porte; plante-o onde a altura adulta, as raízes tabulares, a sombra e o acesso à água sejam compatíveis. É uma escolha à escala da floresta, não uma árvore compacta de pomar.","file":"z-c570e6ca.mp3"},"z-d14646bc":{"text":"Denk in decennia, niet alleen in oogsten. De blauwe quandong kan een grote boom worden; kies een plek waar volwassen hoogte, plankwortels, schaduw en water passen. Dit is een keuze op bosschaal, geen compacte boomgaardboom.","file":"z-d14646bc.mp3"},"z-dbe5bc47":{"text":"A Flora de Nova Gales do Sul associa esta espécie à floresta tropical subtropical de terras baixas e ribeirinha, sobretudo em aluviões e margens de cursos de água. Use essa pista de habitat para pensar na humidade, no movimento do solo e nas outras camadas junto a uma árvore de dossel.","file":"z-dbe5bc47.mp3"},"z-228e27e4":{"text":"De NSW-flora verbindt deze soort met laagland- en beekgebonden subtropisch regenwoud, vooral op alluviale grond en langs waterlopen. Gebruik die habitat als aanleiding om na te denken over vocht, bodem en de andere lagen naast een kroonboom.","file":"z-228e27e4.mp3"},"z-367d3ad5":{"text":"Registos de floresta tropical beschrevem pombos-da-fruta e outras aves a alimentarem-se no dossel. Os frutos caídos também são usados por animais terrestres, incluindo perus-do-mato e casuares. A árvore liga mais do que um estrato da floresta.","file":"z-367d3ad5.mp3"},"z-557070cb":{"text":"Regenwoudbronnen beschrijven fruitduiven en andere vogels die in de kroon eten. Gevallen vruchten worden ook gebruikt door dieren op de bosbodem, waaronder boskalkoenen en kasuarissen. De boom verbindt meerdere boslagen.","file":"z-557070cb.mp3"},"z-75cdad2a":{"text":"No norte de Queensland, os casuares-australianos comem o fruto e podem transportar a semente grande para longe da árvore-mãe antes de a depositarem. É uma relação de dispersão de sementes, não apenas um animal a comer uma colheita. Onde há casuares, são importantes as ligações entre áreas de floresta intacta.","file":"z-75cdad2a.mp3"},"z-95cfcfaf":{"text":"In Noord-Queensland eten zuidelijke kasuarissen de vrucht en kunnen ze het grote zaad meenemen van de moederboom voordat ze het uitscheiden. Dit is zaadverspreiding, niet alleen een dier dat een oogst eet. Waar kasuarissen leven, zijn verbindingen tussen intacte bossen belangrijk.","file":"z-95cfcfaf.mp3"},"z-d27b3f1":{"text":"De blauwe steenvrucht heeft een dunne vruchtlaag rond een harde, diep gegroefde pit; de pit bevat zaden. Het opvallende oppervlak is een beschermende endocarp. Het zaadverhaal begint dus achter de blauwe buitenkant.","file":"z-d27b3f1.mp3"},"z-67a574df":{"text":"De blauwe steenvrucht heeft een dunne vruchtlaag rond een harde, diep gegroefde pit met zaden. Het opvallende oppervlak is een beschermend endocarp. Het zaadverhaal begint dus achter de blauwe buitenkant.","file":"z-67a574df.mp3"},"z-a968996e":{"text":"Zoek naar enkelvoudige, langwerpig-elliptische bladeren met regelmatige tandjes. NSW Flora Online aanvaardt Elaeocarpus grandis en vermeldt dat sommige taxonomische behandelingen deze soort opnemen in het ruimere E. angustifolius-concept. Bewaar bron en regio bij vergelijkingen.","file":"z-a968996e.mp3"},"z-81ecd965":{"text":"Procure folhas simples, oblongas a elípticas e regularmente dentadas. A NSW Flora Online aceita Elaeocarpus grandis e refere que algumas classificações a incluem no conceito mais abrangente de E. angustifolius. Registe a fonte e a região ao comparar dados.","file":"z-81ecd965.mp3"},"z-5fbb4764":{"text":"Um citrino espinhoso do sub-bosque da floresta tropical, com folhas pequenas e distintas, frutos alongados e uma relação documentada com uma borboleta hospedeira.","file":"z-5fbb4764.mp3"},"z-d6422c77":{"text":"Een doornige citrus uit de regenwoudonderlaag, met opvallend kleine bladeren, vingervormige vruchten en een gedocumenteerde relatie met een waardvlinder.","file":"z-d6422c77.mp3"},"z-44a45e85":{"text":"A lima-caviar muda a perspetiva: de uma árvore de dossel ampla para um arbusto espinhoso do sub-bosque da floresta tropical. Acrescente uma camada nativa sob árvores alimentares mais altas e a plantação começa a refletir a estrutura de uma floresta subtropical.","file":"z-44a45e85.mp3"},"z-96d7478f":{"text":"De vingerlimoen verandert het beeld: van een brede kroonboom naar een doornige regenwoudstruik in de onderlaag. Voeg een inheemse laag toe onder hogere voedselbomen en de aanplant gaat de structuur van subtropisch bos weerspiegelen.","file":"z-96d7478f.mp3"},"z-684893e1":{"text":"Na natureza, cresce como arbusto ou pequena árvore; explore um lugar no estrato intermédio ou inferior, com espaço para os ramos espinhosos, luz e acesso. A ideia de desenho é combinar colheita útil com uma camada florestal nativa; a adequação depende do clima local e da cultivar.","file":"z-684893e1.mp3"},"z-dbaf5f31":{"text":"Van nature is het een struik of kleine boom. Verken een plek in de midden- of onderlaag met ruimte voor stekelige takken, licht en toegang. Het ontwerpidee combineert een bruikbare oogst met een inheemse boslaag; de geschiktheid hangt af van klimaat en cultivar.","file":"z-dbaf5f31.mp3"},"z-cf683e4f":{"text":"A lima-caviar é nativa de comunidades de floresta tropical no sudeste de Queensland e nordeste de Nova Gales do Sul. Nessas florestas, é parte de uma comunidade estratificada, não uma cultura isolada. Pergunte que dossel, abrigo e vizinhança o seu local pode oferecer.","file":"z-cf683e4f.mp3"},"z-c3b5cfa3":{"text":"De vingerlimoen is inheems in regenwoudgemeenschappen van Zuidoost-Queensland en Noordoost-New South Wales. In dat bos is de plant deel van een gelaagde gemeenschap, geen op zichzelf staand gewas. Vraag welke kroon, beschutting en buren uw plek kan bieden.","file":"z-c3b5cfa3.mp3"},"z-f20a196a":{"text":"A borboleta-cauda-de-andorinha-graciosa está registada como utilizadora da lima-caviar hospedeira. A lagarta come folhas: a relação é com a planta viva, não com o fruto. Observe antes de podar; algumas folhas roídas podem revelar uma teia alimentar mais ampla, não necessariamente um problema.","file":"z-f20a196a.mp3"},"z-84333765":{"text":"De kleine citrusvlinder is gedocumenteerd als gebruiker van de vingerlimoen als waardplant. De rups eet bladeren: de relatie is met de levende plant, niet met de vrucht. Kijk goed voordat u snoeit; aangevreten bladeren kunnen wijzen op een voedselweb, niet automatisch op schade.","file":"z-84333765.mp3"},"z-4ad4a168":{"text":"O fruto é uma baga cítrica alongada e estreita. No interior, as vesículas de sumo separam-se em pequenas esferas quando o fruto é aberto. O comprimento e as cores da casca e da polpa variam; a forma alongada e as vesículas são a assinatura, não uma vagem cheia de sementes grandes.","file":"z-4ad4a168.mp3"},"z-ff496a75":{"text":"De vrucht is een langwerpige, smalle citrusbes. Binnenin vallen sapblaasjes uiteen in kleine bolletjes wanneer de vrucht wordt geopend. Lengte en schil- en pulpkleur verschillen; de langwerpige vorm en sapblaasjes zijn kenmerkend, geen peul met grote zaden.","file":"z-ff496a75.mp3"},"z-17f614f2":{"text":"Uma planta obtida de semente pode diferir de uma seleção com nome. As orientações de cultivo comercial abordam porta-enxertos de semente e propagação vegetativa; consulte o guia de Nova Gales do Sul para escolher o método adequado ao objetivo. Registe a cultivar, a origem e o método de propagação.","file":"z-17f614f2.mp3"},"z-4a1f9ea0":{"text":"Een zaailing kan afwijken van een benoemde selectie. Teeltrichtlijnen bespreken zaailingonderstammen en vegetatieve vermeerdering; raadpleeg de NSW-gids om de methode bij uw doel te kiezen. Noteer cultivar, herkomst en vermeerderingsmethode.","file":"z-4a1f9ea0.mp3"},"z-22073e11":{"text":"As folhas da lima-caviar são simples, alternadas e muito menores do que as folhas longas de desenhos genéricos de citrinos. A referência mostra a sua forma compacta; os espinhos axilares retos do ramo também ajudam a identificar a espécie. Compare vários rebentos, pois a forma das folhas varia.","file":"z-22073e11.mp3"},"z-9a8680b":{"text":"De bladeren van de vingerlimoen zijn enkelvoudig, staan afwisselend en zijn veel kleiner dan de lange bladeren in algemene citrusillustraties. De referentiefoto toont hun compacte vorm; ook de rechte okselstandige doorns helpen bij herkenning. Vergelijk meerdere scheuten, want bladvorm varieert.","file":"z-9a8680b.mp3"},"z-79189f9e":{"text":"Uma árvore aromática das florestas tropicais de Queensland, cuja floração pode transformar um perfil de planta numa pergunta sobre quem visita as flores e quando.","file":"z-79189f9e.mp3"},"z-3c49c42c":{"text":"Een geurige boom uit de regenwouden van Queensland. De bloei maakt van een plantenprofiel een vraag: wie bezoekt de bloemen, en wanneer?","file":"z-3c49c42c.mp3"},"z-8bd135e3":{"text":"A murta-limão traz uma árvore das florestas costeiras de Queensland para a história da floresta alimentar. Não é apenas “a erva com aroma a limão”: pode formar uma camada lenhosa aromática, um episódio de floração e um convite a observar visitantes sazonais.","file":"z-8bd135e3.mp3"},"z-ea76aa35":{"text":"Citroenmirte brengt een boom uit de kustregenwouden van Queensland in het verhaal van het voedselbos. Het is niet alleen “het kruid met citroengeur”: het kan een geurige houtige laag en bloeimoment vormen, en uitnodigen tot observatie van seizoensbezoekers.","file":"z-ea76aa35.mp3"},"z-c785a0f8":{"text":"A espécie pode crescer como arbusto ou árvore de porte médio. Na floresta alimentar, planeie o tamanho adulto, o abrigo e a humidade de que precisa. Use a folhagem como ligação sensorial sem perder de vista o dossel mais amplo e as plantas vizinhas.","file":"z-c785a0f8.mp3"},"z-154513e1":{"text":"De soort kan uitgroeien tot een middelgrote struik of boom. Plan in het voedselbos voor de volwassen maat, beschutting en vochtbehoefte. Gebruik het blad als zintuiglijke verbinding en houd tegelijk de kroonlaag en naburige planten in beeld.","file":"z-154513e1.mp3"},"z-fcede9f9":{"text":"A murta-limão ocorre naturalmente nas florestas costeiras de Queensland. O Jardim Botânico Nacional Australiano também a cultiva numa ravina abrigada de floresta tropical, protegida pela copa e pelos arbustos circundantes. Poderá a sua floresta alimentar oferecer um local igualmente abrigado e húmido?","file":"z-fcede9f9.mp3"},"z-ebe1ed88":{"text":"De citroenmirte komt van nature voor in de kustbossen van Queensland. De Australian National Botanic Gardens kweekt haar ook in een beschutte regenwoudgeul, met bescherming door de kroon en omringende struiken. Kan uw voedselbos een vergelijkbare beschutte, vochtige plek bieden?","file":"z-ebe1ed88.mp3"},"z-22fba47a":{"text":"Os cachos de flores brancas têm muitos estames evidentes. Em vez de presumir qual espécie local as poliniza, observe um ramo florido em diferentes horas: quem o visita, quanto tempo permanece e a atividade muda com o tempo? Registe as observações com data e local.","file":"z-22fba47a.mp3"},"z-7be62f16":{"text":"De trossen witte bloemen hebben opvallende meeldraden. Ga niet uit van een specifieke lokale bestuiver, maar observeer een bloeiende tak op verschillende momenten: wie komt langs, hoe lang blijft die en verandert de activiteit met het weer? Noteer datum en plek.","file":"z-7be62f16.mp3"},"z-fd5fc70b":{"text":"O fruto é uma cápsula semelhante a uma noz, com várias sementes pequenas. O Jardim Botânico Nacional Australiano refere que as cápsulas podem reter as sementes até o fruto cair. Consulte o guia associado antes de escolher um método de propagação e registe se o material veio de semente ou estaca.","file":"z-fd5fc70b.mp3"},"z-2400f4d":{"text":"De vrucht is een nootachtige capsule met enkele kleine zaden. Volgens de Australian National Botanic Gardens kunnen de capsules het zaad vasthouden tot de vrucht valt. Raadpleeg de teeltgids voordat u een vermeerderingsmethode kiest en noteer of het materiaal uit zaad of een stek kwam.","file":"z-2400f4d.mp3"},"z-5b2eee4b":{"text":"Backhousia citriodora pertence à família das mirtáceas. As folhas, fortemente aromáticas a limão, são ovais a lanceoladas; as flores surgem em cachos pedunculados com numerosos estames plumosos. O nome científico destaca o aroma, mas não substitui a identificação cuidadosa.","file":"z-5b2eee4b.mp3"},"z-88f1053e":{"text":"Backhousia citriodora behoort tot de mirtefamilie. De sterk citroengeurende bladeren zijn ovaal tot lancetvormig; de bloemen staan in gesteelde trossen met talrijke pluizige meeldraden. De wetenschappelijke naam verwijst naar de geur, maar vervangt geen zorgvuldige determinatie.","file":"z-88f1053e.mp3"},"z-3f96d28d":{"text":"Registe os primeiros botões, o pico da floração, a formação das cápsulas e o rebentar das folhas em notas datadas. A planta torna-se um calendário vivo deste local, que pode comparar com a chuva, a temperatura e as espécies vizinhas em flor.","file":"z-3f96d28d.mp3"},"z-bfcbc528":{"text":"Noteer de eerste knoppen, de piek van de bloei, de capsulevorming en de bladgroei in gedateerde notities. Zo wordt de plant een levende kalender voor deze plek, te vergelijken met regen, temperatuur en bloeiende buren.","file":"z-bfcbc528.mp3"},"z-6cf98dca":{"text":"O seu segundo Orbe está pronto. Escolha uma planta australiana da floresta tropical para lhe dar um nome e abrir o seu PIMO.","file":"z-6cf98dca.mp3"},"z-5b3c6620":{"text":"Uw tweede Orb is klaar. Kies een Australische regenwoudplant om hem een naam te geven en zijn PIMO te openen.","file":"z-5b3c6620.mp3"}});
